<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Bold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1433">1433</a>–<a href="/years/1477">1477</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles, Duke of Burgundy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Bold was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1467, ruling the Burgundian lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. He sacked rebellious Liège in 1468, bought Guelders in 1473 and sought a royal crown to link his territories. His wars with the Swiss and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> ended in defeat, and he was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> in 1477, after which his inheritance passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold.jpg" alt="Charles the Bold" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rogier van der Weyden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_the_Bold_1460.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles the Bold</b> created standing companies of ordinance in 1471 and a powerful artillery train, but his army was routed by the Swiss Confederates at the Battle of Grandson and the Battle of Morat in 1476, and he was killed at the Battle of Nancy on 5 January…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy’s greatest military moment came under René II, whose lands lay across the corridor <b>Charles the Bold</b> of Burgundy needed to link his territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Burgundian Wars they defeated <b>Charles the Bold</b> of Burgundy at the Battle of Grandson and the Battle of Murten in 1476 and, as allies of Duke René II of Lorraine, at the Battle of Nancy in 1477, where Charles was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Liège rose again, was defeated at the Battle of Brustem in 1467 and, after a final rising, was taken and sacked by <b>Charles the Bold</b> in 1468; according to tradition six hundred men of Franchimont made a desperate night attack on the duke’s camp before the city fell.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard Taruskin noted that it was a favourite of <b>Charles the Bold</b> and suggested it may have been written for him, but researchers have shown that it was in use before he became Duke of Burgundy.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Charles the Bold</b> died fighting Swiss and Lorrainer troops outside Nancy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
